1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:06,380
By the hundred-and-twentieth night, the sea
came up Market Street to meet it.

2
00:00:06,750 --> 00:00:10,655
The first night, we just
thought it was beautiful.

3
00:00:11,750 --> 00:00:16,870
I was ten. This is what the next
two hundred days were like.

4
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:19,250
Start from the beginning, Miss.

5
00:00:19,620 --> 00:00:23,460
My Nana wrote down the high
tide twice a day.

6
00:00:23,830 --> 00:00:26,775
So had her mother. And hers.

7
00:00:27,145 --> 00:00:31,265
Forty centimetres over the
table. That's not right.

8
00:00:31,635 --> 00:00:34,125
Spring tide, Odile! Put your wellies on!

9
00:00:34,495 --> 00:00:36,860
Not in my book it isn't.

10
00:00:37,230 --> 00:00:41,745
In our kitchen, Mum's pager went
off before the toast did.

11
00:00:42,115 --> 00:00:44,505
She drove the lifeboat.

12
00:00:44,875 --> 00:00:48,740
Nana, why do you still do the book?
The harbour has a computer.

13
00:00:49,110 --> 00:00:53,830
The computer wasn't here in eighteen
eighty-five. The book was.

14
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:56,320
Nana, look. It's bigger.

15
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:02,020
Yes. It is.

16
00:01:02,390 --> 00:01:04,005
NEWS ANCHOR: Good evening.

17
00:01:04,390 --> 00:01:08,630
Tonight the Moon is closer to the Earth
than it has ever been measured.

18
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:13,875
Observatories measure the Moon's distance
every night, with lasers. Tonight it is

19
00:01:13,875 --> 00:01:17,065
thirty-two thousand kilometres
closer than yesterday.

20
00:01:17,435 --> 00:01:19,800
Nobody can yet say why.

21
00:01:20,170 --> 00:01:22,435
Nana? What does closer mean?

22
00:01:22,805 --> 00:01:27,825
The Moon is what lifts the sea, love.
A closer Moon lifts it higher.

23
00:01:28,195 --> 00:01:31,835
That night, Nana wrote in the
book for a long time.

24
00:01:33,405 --> 00:01:35,945
Then she locked it in the bread bin.

25
00:01:36,315 --> 00:01:39,805
On Day 3 she added a column of her own.

26
00:01:40,175 --> 00:01:43,665
A small tide that came when
the Moon wasn't there.

27
00:01:44,035 --> 00:01:47,400
A closer Moon pulls harder on the sea.

28
00:01:48,570 --> 00:01:52,835
Come half as close, and the tide
is eight times higher.

29
00:01:53,205 --> 00:01:57,545
On Day 9, Nana did sums until
the kettle boiled dry.

30
00:01:57,915 --> 00:02:00,105
Nana? It's four in the morning.

31
00:02:00,475 --> 00:02:03,695
Go back to bed, love. I'm nearly done.

32
00:02:04,065 --> 00:02:10,385
It is a high spring tide, and it will
settle. The Board sees no cause for alarm.

33
00:02:10,755 --> 00:02:14,775
It won't settle, Arthur. It
will come up Chapel Hill.

34
00:02:15,145 --> 00:02:18,085
We've heard your floods before, Odile.

35
00:02:18,455 --> 00:02:22,475
They laughed. Nana put on
her hat, and we left.

36
00:02:22,845 --> 00:02:26,710
On Day 12, I found a paper in Nana's coat.

37
00:02:27,080 --> 00:02:30,345
You bought the chapel? Nana,
it hasn’t got a roof!

38
00:02:30,715 --> 00:02:32,755
It's got a good view.

39
00:02:33,125 --> 00:02:37,240
By Day 30, the sea came over
the quay every night.

40
00:02:39,125 --> 00:02:42,940
NEWS ANCHOR: Scientists now expect the
Moon to settle within the week.

41
00:02:43,310 --> 00:02:48,355
It didn't. Every promise on the
news lasted about four days.

42
00:02:48,725 --> 00:02:50,160
Where's my bed?

43
00:02:50,725 --> 00:02:52,970
Up the hill. Airing.

44
00:02:53,340 --> 00:02:56,280
I'm not moving, Nana. This is my house.

45
00:02:56,650 --> 00:02:58,915
I know it is, love.

46
00:02:59,285 --> 00:03:01,750
Even the calendar went wrong.

47
00:03:02,120 --> 00:03:07,710
A closer Moon goes round faster: the
month was down to twenty-two days.

48
00:03:08,080 --> 00:03:11,820
By Day 60 the tides had doubled.

49
00:03:14,080 --> 00:03:17,920
Quay Street was only ours at low water.

50
00:03:18,290 --> 00:03:23,455
And at low tide the sea went out so far,
we could see the harbour floor.

51
00:03:25,290 --> 00:03:28,945
Crabs, bicycles, and somebody's piano.

52
00:03:29,315 --> 00:03:33,480
Mum's lifeboat went out
eleven times that week.

53
00:03:33,850 --> 00:03:37,465
Mostly for people who had gone
back for one more thing.

54
00:03:37,835 --> 00:03:39,495
What's the line for?

55
00:03:40,865 --> 00:03:42,380
For later.

56
00:03:42,865 --> 00:03:48,480
On Day 70, a scientist said the
thing nobody had thought of.

57
00:03:48,850 --> 00:03:54,799
Nothing falls this slowly. The Moon isn't
falling. Something is taking its speed

58
00:03:54,799 --> 00:03:56,955
away, a little on every loop.

59
00:03:57,325 --> 00:04:03,565
Spacecraft do it on purpose: swing past a
planet, and steal a little of its speed.

60
00:04:03,935 --> 00:04:06,850
Something was doing it to our Moon.

61
00:04:07,220 --> 00:04:09,430
So the Moon is being robbed?

62
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:15,195
Of speed. A little every time round.
And a slower Moon swings closer.

63
00:04:15,565 --> 00:04:21,509
We can't see it, because it is a black hole
smaller than a grain of sand, and a

64
00:04:21,509 --> 00:04:23,540
third as heavy as the Moon.

65
00:04:23,910 --> 00:04:26,925
No telescope could find it.

66
00:04:29,910 --> 00:04:35,040
The only things big enough to feel
it were the Moon and the sea.

67
00:04:35,410 --> 00:04:38,550
Nana! A tide with no Moon.
That's your column!

68
00:04:38,920 --> 00:04:39,980
Is it.

69
00:04:40,920 --> 00:04:43,035
She didn't even look up.

70
00:04:43,405 --> 00:04:45,220
Day 90.

71
00:04:49,405 --> 00:04:55,635
The lighthouse was an island twice a day,
and the school moved up the hill.

72
00:04:56,005 --> 00:05:03,663
It wasn't just us. Every port on Earth closed.
Whole cities moved inland, one street

73
00:05:03,663 --> 00:05:04,575
at a time.

74
00:05:04,945 --> 00:05:11,682
In some bays the shape of the coast made it
worse. Places that already had big tides

75
00:05:11,682 --> 00:05:12,965
got the biggest.

76
00:05:13,335 --> 00:05:19,501
So by Day 90, this is what we knew. The Moon
came closer every night. Something we

77
00:05:19,501 --> 00:05:25,065
couldn't see was slowing it down. And
every tide was higher than the last.

78
00:05:25,435 --> 00:05:28,350
What nobody knew was where it would stop.

79
00:05:28,720 --> 00:05:30,885
Miss? Is it going to hit them?

80
00:05:31,255 --> 00:05:34,370
It can't hit, silly. It's
always been right there.

81
00:05:34,740 --> 00:05:36,780
Mum? Is it going to hit us?

82
00:05:37,150 --> 00:05:39,970
No, love. I promise you it can't.

83
00:05:40,340 --> 00:05:46,030
Mum was right. A moon doesn't fall onto
its planet. It goes round it.

84
00:05:47,200 --> 00:05:52,541
And one that came really close would be
pulled to pieces long before it touched.

85
00:05:52,541 --> 00:05:55,745
Ours was never going to come
anywhere near that.

86
00:05:56,115 --> 00:05:59,050
You care more about that book
than about our house!

87
00:05:59,420 --> 00:06:03,710
The book is how I know what's
coming for our house, love.

88
00:06:04,080 --> 00:06:07,545
I didn't speak to her for three days.

89
00:06:10,080 --> 00:06:14,970
On Day 110, I found two pages
she'd glued together.

90
00:06:15,340 --> 00:06:17,555
Her sum from Day 9.

91
00:06:17,925 --> 00:06:22,490
And at the bottom, the last high
tide: thirty-five metres.

92
00:06:22,860 --> 00:06:28,849
And a letter from nineteen ninety-eight. She
had warned them about a flood once

93
00:06:28,849 --> 00:06:29,380
before.

94
00:06:29,750 --> 00:06:33,915
They'd written back that there
was no cause for alarm.

95
00:06:34,285 --> 00:06:37,375
You knew. Since Day 9. Why
didn't you tell them?

96
00:06:37,745 --> 00:06:40,890
I did, love. Day 10. You were there.

97
00:06:41,260 --> 00:06:42,520
They laughed.

98
00:06:43,260 --> 00:06:48,146
They'll believe the sea before they believe
me. So I'm getting the hill ready for

99
00:06:48,146 --> 00:06:48,930
when they do.

100
00:06:49,300 --> 00:06:53,515
On Day 120, the sea came up Market Street.

101
00:06:56,300 --> 00:07:00,590
From the steps, I watched it
reach my bedroom window.

102
00:07:03,300 --> 00:07:06,740
Then I couldn't see my window any more.

103
00:07:07,110 --> 00:07:13,315
My mother carried me up those steps, the
night of the big sea. I was seven.

104
00:07:13,685 --> 00:07:17,755
She told them it was coming.
They said it would settle.

105
00:07:18,125 --> 00:07:21,840
The next day, the sea came
into the Harbour Board.

106
00:07:22,210 --> 00:07:23,800
It will settle.

107
00:07:24,210 --> 00:07:27,230
He still said it. Just quieter.

108
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:33,065
Even the ground rose and fell now.
Two metres, twice a day.

109
00:07:33,600 --> 00:07:37,240
Nobody felt it. Only the cups did.

110
00:07:37,610 --> 00:07:42,875
Mr. Pell. This is my Nana's book. Every
tide for a hundred and forty years.

111
00:07:43,245 --> 00:07:45,225
Wren, not now.

112
00:07:45,595 --> 00:07:50,710
Nana wrote down tonight's tide on Day 9.
Fifteen metres. Go and look at it.

113
00:07:51,080 --> 00:07:53,945
Good Lord. She had it right.

114
00:07:54,315 --> 00:07:56,305
She had all of it right.

115
00:07:56,675 --> 00:07:58,795
Odile. Where does it stop?

116
00:07:59,165 --> 00:08:00,030
There.

117
00:08:01,165 --> 00:08:03,280
Then that's where we go.

118
00:08:03,650 --> 00:08:07,665
Four hundred people slept in
the chapel that night.

119
00:08:08,035 --> 00:08:10,775
Mr. Pell carried the beds.

120
00:08:11,145 --> 00:08:17,385
On Day 160, the scientists finally
had a number of their own.

121
00:08:17,755 --> 00:08:24,315
The visitor gets faster with every loop.
Around Day 200, it will be fast enough to

122
00:08:24,315 --> 00:08:24,875
escape.

123
00:08:25,245 --> 00:08:30,305
And the Moon should stop at about a hundred
and twenty-eight thousand kilometres.

124
00:08:30,675 --> 00:08:32,665
Nana. That's your number.

125
00:08:33,035 --> 00:08:34,775
I rounded up.

126
00:08:35,145 --> 00:08:41,899
By Day 180, the sea was at the churchyard
wall, and the Moon was nearly three times

127
00:08:41,899 --> 00:08:42,550
as wide.

128
00:08:42,920 --> 00:08:48,953
Mum launched the lifeboat one last time, from
a ramp cut into the hill, and brought

129
00:08:48,953 --> 00:08:51,425
a family back from the lighthouse.

130
00:08:51,795 --> 00:08:55,885
That night I saw Nana
writing in the book again.

131
00:08:57,455 --> 00:09:02,745
Not the tide. Just our names.
All four hundred of them.

132
00:09:03,115 --> 00:09:05,505
Nana? What if your sum is wrong?

133
00:09:05,875 --> 00:09:10,670
Then we go up one more step, love.
And I buy a better pencil.

134
00:09:11,040 --> 00:09:13,355
Did it stop, Miss? At the line?

135
00:09:13,725 --> 00:09:15,890
Day 200.

136
00:09:19,725 --> 00:09:24,490
The whole town stood on the chapel
steps and watched it come.

137
00:09:24,860 --> 00:09:28,450
Two steps below the line. One.

138
00:09:29,860 --> 00:09:34,175
It touched the white line. And it stopped.

139
00:09:36,860 --> 00:09:43,361
That night, the visitor made its last loop.
It had stolen all the speed it needed,

140
00:09:43,361 --> 00:09:45,740
and it flew off into the dark.

141
00:09:46,110 --> 00:09:52,636
We thought it would take the Moon with it. It
didn't. It left the Moon exactly where

142
00:09:52,636 --> 00:09:53,180
it was.

143
00:09:53,550 --> 00:09:56,445
Your book now, love. Page one.

144
00:09:56,815 --> 00:09:59,405
Day 1. High tide: on time.

145
00:09:59,775 --> 00:10:03,140
We built the town again, above the line.

146
00:10:03,510 --> 00:10:09,150
The tide still climbs thirty-five
metres, twice a day, and falls back.

147
00:10:09,520 --> 00:10:12,235
Miss? Was the Moon really that small?

148
00:10:19,520 --> 00:10:22,235
Smaller than your thumbnail, love.

149
00:10:22,605 --> 00:10:28,075
I'm eighty now. The same age Nana
was when she gave me the book.

150
00:10:28,445 --> 00:10:32,840
People ask if I miss the old Moon. I do.

151
00:10:36,445 --> 00:10:40,935
But nobody who has seen this one
rise would give it back.

152
00:10:41,305 --> 00:10:45,900
What would you carry up the hill
first? Tell us in the comments.
